On March 29th, I purchased this book through the pre-order method for RMB 31 and displayed that it would be pushed to my device on the same day. However, at this time, Amazon's website still could not preview this book (since it had not been published).
On March 31st, book content pushed to the devices is empty; there is only "please expect". Meanwhile, Amazon has priced the electronic version down to 18 Yuan.
In these two days I've tried repeatedly to delete and then re-synchronize my downloaded empty book on the device, but I still cannot get the electronic version of "The Nature of Business" which contains actual content; however, I was able to preview the book on Amazon's website.
I have several thoughts to share at this moment.
1. The price issue is not really the most important thing; what’s more crucial is how to turn Kindle’s blank books into ones with actual content rather than always downloading “coming soon.”
2. The books pre-ordered should not be bought again because it would be very complicated to re-download an empty book if they were cancelled.
3. I hope Amazon provides a normal channel to report such issues; when there is any doubt about downloading or using e-books, one can have an outlet to feedback (e.g., through tickets or online customer service). This can be similar to how Microsoft’s official store handles it.
Lonely little lion
When I got the book, I thought it would be as simple as The Lost Piece of Paper, but turns out there's still a lot of text!
After reading it three times, I finally read this book to the children, a little lion that can shoot guns and one that loves juice-flavored lollipops.
When seeing a lion eat its hunter, there is some unease in one’s heart, for modern urbanites find it difficult to accept such an arrangement involving animal predation.
In the end, this little lion who succeeded in human society has lost himself; he is no longer entirely a lion nor an entirely human being.
Finally, all that is left of the little lion is his silhouette, and he has no idea where he went.
Perhaps in years to come, the image of this little lion will still linger in the mind of a grown-up child who remembers it, and she may be touched or inspired on some random day.
