A while ago I read and just happened to see something scribbled from memory about some reflections on my reading.
This is more of an anthology of love fragments from everyday life than a collection of short stories.
The tone of this book is extremely mild and mundane – like drinking plain water – to an extreme level of brevity and economy in choosing words and sentences, as well as in constructing the plot, which resembles a quiet street on a summer afternoon.
Every short story is a small snapshot of time from ordinary life, with no beginning and no end.
Finally gathered together, they named it "When We Talk About Love, What Are We Talking About?"
I think this collection of novels and its title represent some kind of performative art form; in their author’s view, love is not merely melodrama on a stage or the immortality described in urban legends, nor does it have to be bound by your adoration for me and my devotion to you. Love should also not be restricted to above-noted circumstances; it should be more than these; love should be a life without drama or grandiose aspirations, a monotonous daily grind that lacks purpose but is filled with despair over one’s inability to control reality, an escape from reality, and confusion about the future.
As you read this book, memories of arguing couples beside you, uninteresting marriages with acquaintances, and unsatisfactory relationships may come to mind.
This is precisely why I do not recommend this book, from an aesthetic standpoint; its writing style and plot are uninteresting unless your life has been as tumultuous or sweet as living in a sugar castle, then reading becomes a matter of familiarity with boredom.
And in terms of depth of thought, everyday situations do not surprise or expand one’s mind and perspective (this is unlike in Gabriel Garcia Marquez’s Love in the Time of Cholera), while brief episodes fail to address any issues, such as what kind of mistake led to the failure of love?
What causes the breakdown of the protagonist's marriage?
So I’m not going to recommend this book anyway; it’s so trivial that either way you read it doesn’t make much difference.
Author's ideas are expressed more through skills and form than plot, which is also the reason for a poorer reading experience with this book.
